<!--
    GH-8500: "stack address taken, stored, then dereferenced is mis-analysed."
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/8500

    Source (gcc -O0):
      int identity(int x) {
        int *p = &x;     // take the address of the (stack-resident) parameter
        *p = x;          // store THROUGH the pointer back into the stack slot
        return *p;       // read THROUGH the pointer
      }

    The address of the parameter's stack slot (s-0x1c) is spilled to another
    stack slot (s-0x10 = p).  The value is then stored through p (*p = x) and
    re-read through p (return *p).  RuleStoreVarnode turns the store into a
    stack COPY (Varnode::stack_store) one main-loop iteration before
    RuleLoadVarnode turns the aliasing LOAD into a read of s-0x1c; dead-code
    removal takes the unread store in between, heritage then finds the new
    read with no reaching definition ("Heritage AFTER dead removal"), the
    deadcode-delay restart does not close the gap, and the stock decompiler
    prints an uninitialized local:

        xunknown4 identity(void)
        {
          xunknown4 xStack_1c;
          return xStack_1c;
        }

    Port of kuna's option stackalias (kuna_stackalias.rs prepare/holds_store;
    GayHydra #477).  ActionDeadCode::stackAliasPrepare gives every explicit
    stack store in the local frame that a stack pointer could still observe
    (AliasChecker, not Varnode::nolocalalias, not provably overwritten before
    an observer) an autolive hold before the consume walk, and
    ActionVarnodeProps leaves that hold alone, so the store survives until the
    LOAD resolves to it.  Default off, as in kuna: the policy is conservative
    and retains dead frame writes.  With the option on the fork prints

        xunknown4 identity(xunknown4 param_1)
        {
          xunknown4 axStack_1c [3];
          xunknown4 *pxStack_10;
          pxStack_10 = axStack_1c;
          axStack_1c[0] = param_1;
          return param_1;
        }

    i.e. the parameter is recovered and returned; the two retained stores are
    the held (correct but dead) frame writes.

    Assertions follow kuna's testcase.  kuna's heritage already fixed its own
    copy of this bug, so kuna expects both halves to return the parameter; the
    fork reproduces the bug by default, so the first half asserts the stock
    symptom (one "return <type>Stack_NN;") and the second half asserts the
    fixed form.  kuna's register name a0 is the fork's param_1.

    Disassembly (identity @ 0x1139):
      1139 55              PUSH RBP
      113a 4889e5          MOV  RBP,RSP
      113d 897dec          MOV  dword ptr [RBP + -0x14],EDI
      1140 488d45ec        LEA  RAX,[RBP + -0x14]
      1144 488945f8        MOV  qword ptr [RBP + -0x8],RAX
      1148 8b55ec          MOV  EDX,dword ptr [RBP + -0x14]
      114b 488b45f8        MOV  RAX,qword ptr [RBP + -0x8]
      114f 8910            MOV  dword ptr [RAX],EDX
      1151 488b45f8        MOV  RAX,qword ptr [RBP + -0x8]
      1155 8b00            MOV  EAX,dword ptr [RAX]
      1157 5d              POP  RBP
      1158 c3              RET
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1139" readonly="true">
